1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
Two ways to count ions you can't see:

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:05,600
a titration that counts the silver it takes,

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,600
and a precipitate you can weigh.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,560
Exam questions on both test the same few

5
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:11,360
moves.

6
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,840
Use the right data, choose the right mole

7
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,880
ratio, keep track of any dilution, and say

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,320
which way an error pushes the answer.

9
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:22,480
Here are three HSC-style questions.

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:24,800
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

11
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:25,680
through it together.

12
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:28,780
Question one.

13
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:32,020
A ten millilitre sample of seawater is diluted

14
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:34,740
to two hundred and fifty millilitres.

15
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:37,780
Twenty five millilitre aliquots are titrated with silver

16
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:40,020
nitrate, using chromate indicator.

17
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,140
The titres are on screen.

18
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:45,540
Calculate the chloride concentration in the seawater, in

19
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:46,940
grams per litre.

20
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:48,660
Pause here to answer.

21
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:01,306
Start with the data.

22
00:01:01,547 --> 00:01:03,946
The rough titre is only a guide, so

23
00:01:04,187 --> 00:01:04,826
leave it out.

24
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:07,306
The other three agree within a tenth of

25
00:01:07,387 --> 00:01:10,586
a millilitre, so they're concordant, and their mean

26
00:01:10,667 --> 00:01:13,066
is ten point nine zero millilitres.

27
00:01:13,707 --> 00:01:14,746
Now the ratio.

28
00:01:15,307 --> 00:01:18,266
Silver and chloride form silver chloride, one to

29
00:01:18,387 --> 00:01:18,666
one.

30
00:01:19,147 --> 00:01:22,186
The chromate only signals the end, so its

31
00:01:22,267 --> 00:01:24,186
two to one ratio isn't the one you

32
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:24,746
want.

33
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:29,806
Moles of silver:

34
00:01:30,367 --> 00:01:33,646
point zero five zero zero times point zero

35
00:01:33,727 --> 00:01:37,006
one zero nine zero litres, five point four

36
00:01:37,167 --> 00:01:39,966
five times ten to the minus four moles.

37
00:01:40,207 --> 00:01:42,766
That's the chloride in one twenty five millilitre

38
00:01:42,927 --> 00:01:43,686
aliquot.

39
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:46,766
The flask held two hundred and fifty millilitres,

40
00:01:46,847 --> 00:01:49,166
ten times as much, so it held five

41
00:01:49,327 --> 00:01:51,486
point four five times ten to the minus

42
00:01:51,607 --> 00:01:52,526
three moles.

43
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:54,766
All of that came from the ten millilitres

44
00:01:54,807 --> 00:01:55,846
of seawater.

45
00:01:56,127 --> 00:01:59,406
Divide by point zero one zero zero litres:

46
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:02,126
point five four five moles per litre.

47
00:02:02,327 --> 00:02:05,246
Times thirty five point four five, about nineteen

48
00:02:05,327 --> 00:02:07,246
point three grams per litre.

49
00:02:07,327 --> 00:02:09,886
That's close to typical seawater, a good sense

50
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:10,366
check.

51
00:02:12,367 --> 00:02:15,206
The classic slip is stopping at the aliquot.

52
00:02:15,487 --> 00:02:18,126
Divide the moles by twenty five millilitres, and

53
00:02:18,207 --> 00:02:20,446
you get point zero two one eight moles

54
00:02:20,527 --> 00:02:21,566
per litre.

55
00:02:21,647 --> 00:02:24,926
That's the diluted solution, not the seawater.

56
00:02:25,087 --> 00:02:27,486
Ask which liquid the question is about, then

57
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:29,566
scale back up through the dilution.

58
00:02:31,567 --> 00:02:32,726
Question two.

59
00:02:33,087 --> 00:02:36,166
Water treatment plants use aluminium sulfate.

60
00:02:36,447 --> 00:02:39,726
To check a sample's purity, three portions, each

61
00:02:39,807 --> 00:02:42,926
point four zero zero grams, are analysed by

62
00:02:43,047 --> 00:02:45,166
precipitating barium sulfate.

63
00:02:45,487 --> 00:02:47,726
The masses are on screen, and the third

64
00:02:47,807 --> 00:02:49,886
was weighed after only one drying.

65
00:02:50,527 --> 00:02:53,886
Justify which result to exclude, then calculate the

66
00:02:53,967 --> 00:02:56,526
percentage of aluminium sulfate in the sample.

67
00:02:57,087 --> 00:02:58,446
Pause here to answer.

68
00:03:09,667 --> 00:03:10,386
Part a.

69
00:03:10,467 --> 00:03:14,066
Trial three is the highest, but being different

70
00:03:14,227 --> 00:03:15,946
isn't a reason on its own.

71
00:03:16,227 --> 00:03:18,626
The reason is in the method: it was

72
00:03:18,707 --> 00:03:21,826
weighed after only one drying, so it probably

73
00:03:21,947 --> 00:03:22,946
still held water.

74
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:26,546
Extra water means extra mass, so it reads

75
00:03:26,627 --> 00:03:27,186
too high.

76
00:03:27,827 --> 00:03:30,066
Trials one and two were dried to constant

77
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:32,866
mass, and they agree, so their mean, point

78
00:03:33,027 --> 00:03:35,026
seven eight three grams, is the value to

79
00:03:35,107 --> 00:03:35,666
use.

80
00:03:39,667 --> 00:03:40,386
Part b.

81
00:03:40,867 --> 00:03:43,986
Moles of barium sulfate: point seven eight three

82
00:03:44,307 --> 00:03:46,466
over two hundred and thirty three point three

83
00:03:46,547 --> 00:03:49,346
nine, three point three five times ten to

84
00:03:49,427 --> 00:03:50,786
the minus three moles.

85
00:03:51,347 --> 00:03:52,466
Now the ratio.

86
00:03:52,707 --> 00:03:56,386
Each aluminium sulfate holds three sulfate ions, so

87
00:03:56,427 --> 00:03:58,506
it makes three barium sulfates.

88
00:03:58,707 --> 00:04:01,586
Divide by three: one point one two times

89
00:04:01,667 --> 00:04:03,746
ten to the minus three moles of aluminium

90
00:04:03,827 --> 00:04:04,386
sulfate.

91
00:04:04,787 --> 00:04:06,786
Times three hundred and forty two point one

92
00:04:07,027 --> 00:04:09,946
four, that's point three eight three grams.

93
00:04:10,147 --> 00:04:12,706
Out of point four zero zero grams, about

94
00:04:12,787 --> 00:04:13,986
ninety six percent.

95
00:04:14,147 --> 00:04:16,146
That's under a hundred, which makes sense for

96
00:04:16,187 --> 00:04:16,946
a purity.

97
00:04:18,967 --> 00:04:21,686
The classic slip is using one to one.

98
00:04:21,927 --> 00:04:23,926
That gives about two hundred and eighty seven

99
00:04:24,087 --> 00:04:27,206
percent, which is impossible for a purity.

100
00:04:27,287 --> 00:04:29,686
When a percentage comes out over a hundred,

101
00:04:29,767 --> 00:04:31,286
check your ratio first.

102
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:34,180
Question three.

103
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:37,220
A teacher makes up a sodium chloride solution

104
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:40,740
of known concentration, point zero six two zero

105
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:42,260
moles per litre.

106
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:44,660
A student measures it twice.

107
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:47,700
A Mohr titration, with the burette rinsed only

108
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:50,820
with distilled water, gives point zero six three

109
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:51,620
five.

110
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:54,820
A gravimetric analysis, where some silver chloride went

111
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:57,220
through a small tear in the filter paper,

112
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:00,020
gives point zero six zero seven.

113
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,660
Explain why each result differs from the known

114
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:03,620
value.

115
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,260
Then evaluate the claim that their mean shows

116
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:08,260
the analysis was accurate.

117
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:09,700
Pause here to answer.

118
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:21,940
Method one.

119
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:24,500
Water left in the burette dilutes the silver

120
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:28,020
nitrate, so each millilitre carries less silver than

121
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:29,060
the label says.

122
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:31,300
You need a bigger titre to reach the

123
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:32,260
endpoint.

124
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:36,100
But the calculation still uses the label's concentration,

125
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:39,060
so it counts more silver, and more chloride,

126
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:40,100
than was really there.

127
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:42,580
That's why the result is too high.

128
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:46,180
Rinsing with the silver nitrate itself fixes it,

129
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,500
because any liquid left behind is then the

130
00:05:48,660 --> 00:05:49,700
same solution.

131
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:52,580
Method two.

132
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:55,620
Silver chloride that slips through the tear never

133
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:56,820
reaches the balance.

134
00:05:57,300 --> 00:06:00,180
Less mass means fewer moles of silver chloride,

135
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:01,460
so less chloride.

136
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:03,540
That result is too low.

137
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:06,180
Each fault would push the result the same

138
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,900
way every time you repeated it, so both

139
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:09,940
are systematic.

140
00:06:11,967 --> 00:06:12,686
Part b.

141
00:06:12,767 --> 00:06:15,646
The mean lands near the known value only

142
00:06:15,807 --> 00:06:18,046
because one error pushed up and the other

143
00:06:18,127 --> 00:06:20,046
pushed down by a similar amount.

144
00:06:20,527 --> 00:06:22,526
That's luck, not accuracy.

145
00:06:23,167 --> 00:06:25,566
Neither method was accurate, and with a slightly

146
00:06:25,727 --> 00:06:27,646
bigger tear, the mean would miss.

147
00:06:28,287 --> 00:06:31,566
The fixes remove each cause: rinse the burette

148
00:06:31,647 --> 00:06:34,366
with the silver nitrate solution, and filter through

149
00:06:34,447 --> 00:06:35,726
an undamaged paper.

150
00:06:36,207 --> 00:06:37,966
So the claim isn't supported.

151
00:06:38,447 --> 00:06:41,646
To check accuracy properly, run each fixed method

152
00:06:41,727 --> 00:06:43,566
on a known solution like this one.

153
00:06:44,047 --> 00:06:46,046
The trap is trusting the average.

154
00:06:46,447 --> 00:06:49,006
A close average doesn't show that either method

155
00:06:49,087 --> 00:06:49,886
was accurate.

156
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:55,740
So for any precipitation titration or gravimetric question.

157
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:58,860
Leave out rough and faulty results, and say

158
00:06:58,940 --> 00:06:59,580
why.

159
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:02,220
Take the ratio from the precipitate's equation or

160
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:04,660
formula, not from the indicator.

161
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:07,900
Track every dilution back to the original sample.

162
00:07:08,220 --> 00:07:10,540
And for each error, ask whether it adds

163
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:13,740
or loses volume or mass, then say which

164
00:07:13,820 --> 00:07:15,220
way the answer moves.

165
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:19,660
Next, identifying ions: which tests tell you which

166
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:20,940
cation is there?

167
00:07:22,967 --> 00:07:24,406
Here are the key notes.

168
00:07:24,647 --> 00:07:26,566
Pause the video if you need more time

169
00:07:26,647 --> 00:07:27,446
to copy them.
